rtainment industry i love  surfing i love snowboarding i love  traveling i love camping i love nature  there's a lot of things i enjoy  um i love working out um i still love  dancing um  because if it was just entertainment i  probably would feel like i'm waiting  around a lot  but um yeah the post-production uh in  the sure process was the hardest thing  i've ever done i thought the filming was  hard but post-production was just really  hard  we started in new york  and then unfortunately my very dear  friend and one of the investors passed  away  um who i was living with are we shared  in the apartment so that just threw me  off  the older crib it was just really  really upsetting  and i had to move out the hou of the  apartment and i had to start all over  again in l.a and then covert happened  and then it was just the world turned  upside down and i was just like what the  hell our editor got sent back to  australia  with the film footage and it was just  like  it was a disaster  and um it was very frustrating  but i think i had to go through that it  was one of these things where um  i didn't really  know how to navigate post-production and  now i have a much better idea so i'm  kind of grateful for the challenges in a  way even though looking like looking  back on it was an absolute nightmare but  i'm kind of grateful i had to go through  that because now i have a much deeper  understanding and knowledge  of the whole post-production process the  tax credits  the coloring the sound design all those  different elements that i didn't really  understand i i had to just i had to  almost teach myself on the job  so if you approach it as a director  again what things do you think you'll  implement like getting more coverage of  something or knowing like you can't  necessarily always fix it in post sort  of the cliche saying that  i would um 100 have a post production  supervisor on from the beginning whilst  shooting  so then you can prep the footage  and have everything ready for post don't  bring the post-production um  and also the post-production supervisor  should be looking at the budget because  a lot of the time the line producer  won't necessarily put the right amounts  of money  and allocate always because they kind of  once the the movie's finished the line  producer normally leaves and then you've  got your post-production supervisor and  most of the time you've not got enough  money for post so if you have a  post-production supervisor from the  start  you're gonna have a much smoother  journey and like you know shooting the  movie is only the third of the process  so if you have a post-production  supervisor and i've got a great one so  i'm gonna use them again if you'll have  me um i i just think it'd be a much  smoother journey you'll be much more  organized  um  and yeah a lot i've heard a lot of  people have a nightmare on post and it's  unfortunate a lot of people take  advantage of filmmakers in post because  they know that you have to that you're  you're kind of stuck  so it's a very unfortunate situation  because a lot of indie movie makers end  up getting they have an amazing product  and then post they don't have the money  to quite finish it to where the standard  they should be getting it  we were so lucky we got um great people  color we've got amazing sound design  we're amazing composers i mean i really  did luck out for the budget we we got  really good people involved so i'm so  grateful that  i just um  we just managed to get people that  believed in the film and  a much higher level than what we  probably would get for that budget we  got very lucky i mean maybe it was  because of covid i don't know  how do you decide which movie ideas to  work on and which to throw away  good question um  for me i'm kind of turned on by  movies that  talk about areas of life that aren't  normally spoken about as much  um and that i feel people need to know  about  so  i'm  interested in talking about areas of 